<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hesiod</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 700 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hesiod was a Greek poet of around 700 BCE who lived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ascra">Ascra</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a>, where his father had settled after leaving Cyme in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeolis">Aeolis</a>. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theogony">Theogony</a> describes the origins of the gods and the world, and his Works and Days gives advice on farming, work and justice. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> he was regarded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> as a founder of their poetry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hesiod.jpg" alt="Hesiod" width="320" height="551" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yair Haklai · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Hesiod_(%3F)-Ancient_Roman_busts_in_the_Neues_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
